  :project autopax

  |title See for yourself whether you're getting the behavior you want

  |quote
    My hope is that you'll be able to see for yourself whether or not you are getting the behavior you want instead of me needing to tell you every single time.

  |occasion
    While building tooling, on what he wants the tooling to do for the agent using it.

  |about
    The design criterion the estate keeps re-deriving, stated in one sentence: the goal
    of an instrument is to close the agent's own feedback loop, not to carry the
    steward's judgment to it. It also states the standard by which a *correction* is
    working — if the same correction keeps needing to be given, the fix is an
    instrument, not a firmer instruction.

  |before-acting
    - You are about to ask whether the output is right instead of checking.
    - You are giving the same correction to an agent more than once.
    - You are building a tool and deciding what it should report.
    - You are relying on review to catch what you could verify now.

  |see
    - #prx-act-forcing-question
    - #exm-revel-in-the-check

